course: everyday
lesson: 332
title: Going to the doctor and staying together in crowds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A doctor cannot feel your pain, so the most useful thing you can do at the doctor is to say clearly where it hurts, how it feels and when it started. «It hurts here, on the right side of my tummy, since yesterday» helps far more than «I feel bad».
explain: In a big crowd, at a festival, a parade or a football match, it is easy to lose sight of your family in seconds. So you hold an adult's hand and, before you go in, agree a meeting point, a place you can both find if you get separated.
explain: A good meeting point is easy to see and does not move, like a big sign, the entrance gate or the information tent, not a person or an ice-cream van that can drive away.
fact: Tell the doctor clearly where it hurts; it helps them find out what is wrong.
fact: Doctors also need to know how the pain feels and when it started.
fact: Pointing to the exact place that hurts is a good way to show the doctor.
fact: A doctor cannot feel your pain, so they rely on what you tell them.
fact: In a big crowd at a festival or match it is easy to get lost.
fact: In a crowd, hold an adult's hand.
fact: Before going into a crowd, agree a meeting point with your family.
fact: A good meeting point is easy to find and does not move, such as a big sign or the entrance gate.
fact: If you get separated, go to the meeting point and wait there, or ask a worker or police officer for help.
exercise: At the doctor's, what is the most helpful thing a child can do? | Tell the doctor clearly where it hurts | Say «I just feel bad» and nothing more | Stay quiet and let the doctor guess | Let a friend explain it instead
exercise: Why should you tell the doctor where it hurts? | It helps the doctor find out what is wrong | Doctors cannot examine children without it | It makes the visit shorter for the next patient | Doctors write it down only for school
exercise: A boy has an earache. Which is the clearest thing to tell the doctor? | «My left ear hurts inside, since last night» | «Everything is a bit wrong, I don't know where» | «I don't want to say» | «My friend had something like this once»
exercise: Why can a doctor not just know where you hurt? | The doctor cannot feel your pain | Doctors only ever look at the throat | Pain never shows on the outside of you | Doctors are not allowed to touch children
exercise: Besides where it hurts, what else helps a doctor? | How it feels and when it started | Your favourite food and drink | Which class you are in | What you watched on TV
exercise: A girl in Sydney cannot find words for her pain. What can she do? | Point to the exact place that hurts | Say nothing and hope it all goes away | Make up a different place that is easier to say | Ask to leave before the doctor checks
exercise: A family goes to a big music festival. Why should a child stay close to an adult? | It is easy to get lost in a big crowd | Festivals do not allow children to walk alone | Adults need help carrying the bags | Crowds always move very slowly
exercise: Walking through a busy crowd at a football match, what should a child do? | Hold an adult's hand | Walk a few steps ahead to find the way | Follow any family going the same way | Run ahead to get the best seats
exercise: What should a family agree before going into a big crowd? | A meeting point in case they get separated | Who will carry the most snacks and drinks for everyone | Which songs they want to hear | What time they will have lunch
exercise: Which is the best meeting point at a fair? | The big sign at the main entrance | The ice-cream van parked near the rides | The man selling balloons | Wherever the family last stood
exercise: Why is a moving thing, like a food truck, a bad meeting point? | It may move, so you cannot find it again | Food trucks are always too busy | Children are not allowed near food trucks | It is too easy to find
exercise: A boy at a parade in Chicago loses his parents. What should he do? | Go to the agreed meeting point or ask a worker or police officer for help | Wander around the whole parade, shouting their names until he finds them himself | Leave the parade and walk home alone | Go with the first stranger who offers to help
test: A girl tells the nurse «My arm hurts». The nurse asks, «Where exactly?». Why does the nurse ask? | The exact place helps find out what is wrong | Nurses must ask three questions at every visit | The nurse was not listening the first time | Every arm pain is the same anyway
test: Which child is helping the doctor most? | «It hurts right here when I swallow, since Monday» | «I'm a bit poorly, I think, maybe since the weekend» | «It's nothing, really» | «Ask my mum, she knows»
test: A family reaches the gates of a crowded county fair in Texas. What is the smartest first step? | Agree a meeting point, like the main gate | Split up to see more rides quickly | Let the children go ahead alone | Decide to phone each other only if needed
test: What makes a good meeting point in a crowd? | Easy to see and staying in one place | Quiet and hidden away from the busy crowd | Close to where all the food is being cooked | Changing place each hour along with the show
test: A boy lets go of his dad's hand at a busy match to look at a flag. What could happen? | He could get lost in the crowd in seconds | Nothing, because crowds always stay quite still | His dad can always see him over the whole crowd | The crowd will bring him back to his seat later
test: A girl gets separated from her mum at a festival, and they agreed to meet at the information tent. What should she do? | Go to the information tent and wait there | Keep running between stages to look | Go back to the car park alone | Wait for her mum to call her name
test: Why might a doctor ask, «When did the pain start?» | Knowing when it began helps find the cause | Doctors like to check your memory is working well | It decides how long the visit lasts | It is only for writing the date on a form
test: At a crowded parade, which is the safest way for a five-year-old to stay with her family? | Holding an adult's hand | Walking by herself just behind | Sitting on the kerb until it ends | Following the music truck
explain: A girl in Australia got separated from her family at a big match; because they had agreed to meet by the main gate sign, she went there and they found her in minutes.
explain: A boy in Ohio told the doctor his leg only hurt «a bit» because he was scared of an X-ray; it was broken, and it took longer to heal because it was found late.
fact: Pain that is hidden or played down can mean an injury is found late and heals worse.
fact: An X-ray is a special photo of the inside of the body, such as the bones; having one does not hurt.
fact: Most lost children in crowds are found quickly when the family has agreed a meeting point.
test: leg hurts loads but dont wanna say, scared of xray | Tell the doctor the truth; X-rays don't hurt | Say it's fine | Say it hurts a little | Run off
test: lost dad at the festival, we said meet at the big flag | Go to the big flag and wait | Look everywhere for him till you find him | Leave the festival | Follow the crowd out
test: festival's packed, can i go to the stall on my own | Hold an adult's hand; it's easy to get lost | Go, it's close | Go if you're quick | Go and phone later
test: did u hear a kid told the doctor his tummy "was fine" cos he was scared of jabs and "it turned out his appendix burst". lesson? | Tell the doctor the truth about your pain; hidden pain is found late | Play it down so you can go home quicker and skip the jabs and the waiting | Doctors can feel your pain | Only say it hurts if it's bleeding
test: my mate says at the festival "50 kids got lost and some were never found". what's prob exaggerated | Never found; most lost children are found quickly with a meeting point | That festivals get busy | That kids get lost | That meeting points help
test: kids say the girl who got lost at the match "is too dumb to stay with her mum". pass it on? | No, it's easy to get lost in a crowd; don't spread it | Yes, she's dumb | Yes, and tease her about it | Yes, it's funny
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a good meeting point? | Easy to find and fixed, such as a big sign or the entrance gate | A moving vehicle, such as an ice cream van or a ride that travels about the fair | A particular person in the crowd | Any spot chosen after becoming lost
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a patient tells the doctor how the pain feels and when it started. | The doctor cannot feel the pain and relies upon what is said | Doctors must record the details in full for the purposes of insurance | It shortens the appointment | It replaces the examination
exam: It is given that a child has become separated in a crowd. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Go to the meeting point and wait, or ask a worker or police officer | Search the crowd by running about from one end to the other | Leave the venue alone | Follow any adult who offers help
exam: State the effect of hiding or playing down pain. | An injury may be found late and heal worse | It makes an injury heal faster | It has no effect on treatment | It makes the doctor more careful
